{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"answers\":[{\"question_id\":\"Q012_not_true_family_specific_pair\",\"answer\":\"No, not in the evolutionary-homology sense. In SCOPe 2.08, d1s9za_ resolves to domain SUNID 98768 with SCCS k.40.1.1 and belongs under the protein node Amyloidogenic design-1, SUNID 103785; d1hqja_ resolves to domain SUNID 61142 with the same SCCS k.40.1.1 but belongs under a different protein node, Unnamed design-1, SUNID 100906. The two protein nodes share family SUNID 100905, Designed trimeric coiled-coil, whose ancestors are the same-named superfamily k.40.1, fold k.40, and class k, Designed proteins. That shared SCOPe family is therefore a designed-protein structural grouping, not evidence that the two synthetic peptides descend from a common natural ancestor. Do not transfer sequence homology, natural function, or ortholog/paralog status between Amyloidogenic design-1 and Unnamed design-1 just because they share k.40.1.1; at most, SCOPe supports treating them as same-family designed trimeric coiled-coil structural templates with the designed-protein caveat.\",\"evidence_used\":[\"/api/v1\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1s9za_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103786\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103785\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1hqja_\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64668\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100906\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100905\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100904\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100903\",\"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/58788\"]}]}"}}
